{
  "name": "acen_item_space",
  "description": "Where the transform gizmo's action centre sits, and what its basis is, when the edited layer carries a non-identity item transform. Frozen from a measured reference capture; no external editor is consulted at test runtime. The stand is deliberate: the selected quad is irregular so its vertex mean and its bounding-box mid are different points, it sits off the item origin so 'world origin', 'item origin' and 'selection centre' are three distinct points, and the non-uniform item scale INVERTS the selected face's largest-extent axis (local X 1.7 / Z 1.2 -> world X 3.4 / Z 3.6) so a basis derived before the item matrix and one derived after elect a different axis.",
  "provenance": {
    "schema": 1,
    "source": "live-capture",
    "reference": "ref-editor@11.2v3",
    "method": "published action-centre point + axis frame, one cell per (item transform x mode); plus an indirect leg where a numeric uniform scale is applied and the pivot it actually used is solved back out of the moved LAYER-SPACE vertices",
    "captured_utc": "2026-08-09",
    "task": "0648",
    "notes": "The centre column is a WORLD point on the reference side. The indirect column is in the LAYER's own coordinates, which is the only column the two engines must agree on for the geometry to land in the same place: 'origin' prints (0,0,0) on both sides and means two different points, so the published triple alone cannot decide it."
  },
  "units": {
    "center": "world-space point",
    "rot": "degrees (see rotationNote)",
    "matrix": "column-major 4x4, p_world = M * p_local"
  },
